WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.190 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the silence felt like it stretched to  infinity  and my face was beat red frozen like a  deer in headlights i stammered for words  finally i heard the voice of my general  partner come in and swoop in to save me  like a remote and distant loudspeaker  from some far off planet bringing me  back to the present to the sandhill road  conference room to the perfect square of  california sunshine in front of me  nina is not an executive assistant  she is an associate partner at this firm  a huge wave of relief washed over me and  i let out the breath i didn't even  realize i had been holding  i tell you guys a story because it is  literally one of the most cringe-worthy  moments of my entire life but also it  because it is the moment  that i felt like an absolute outsider in  venture capital  i felt like an outsider because i was a  22 year old female in this old boys club  of venture capital and the truth was i  probably was never going to fit in  today only sixteen percent of venture  capital investors are women  and only three percent of all of venture  capital in america goes to female ceos  this is crazy to me  my experiences as a recovering venture  capitalist and now as the founder of my  own  y commenter back startup  are just incredibly difficult for me to  talk about  because i have gone through all of the  stages of grief about how capital is  allocated in our country  denial  anger just so much anger and rage  how is it that this group of just  incredibly well-meaning people  who are my friends who are my mentors  could yield such systematically unequal  outcomes how is it that an industry that  is considered by most to be the most  meritocratic in the world  has portfolio  that look the way that they do  and finally i circled back to optimism  optimism because all of the amazing  female founders that i've met here at  stanford and all the amazing female  investors i know are going to go on to  change things in venture capital  i'm here to help you skip all of those  stages of grief that i went through and  jump to that optimism part as well  i want to start by just talking about  some of the biggest reasons i think  exist for this persistent gap in funding  the first  is the pipeline problem but probably not  in the way that you think  yes women only get 20 of the computer  science degrees in this country  but the real problem is that we are  using that as a pipeline to figure out  who gets funded  this outdated investment criteria that  technical chops are somehow a  prerequisite for being a startup ceo is  absolute [ __ ]  we need to get rid of these archetypes  we have in our head of what an ideal  startup founder looks like  having moved to the operator side now i  can tell you there is an enormous  laundry list of things that you need to  be  and great ceo  nowhere on that list is knowing how to  write a single line of code and we need  to make sure that women are not opting  themselves out because they don't check  off all of these boxes  the second which i know all too well is  the lack of diversity in the decision  makers in this country  it's funny  how the people that  you happen to trust just look a hell of  a lot like you and all of those original  early stage venture capital decisions  are built on trust  one thing i learned in venture capital  is that sourcing is often playing  basketball with your harvard fraternity  brothers  the people that you trust  just happen to look a hell of a lot like  you  maybe they went to the same schools as  you maybe they were in the same  fraternity  you have to check those types of things  the last is unconscious bias  in an hbs study published in 2017  researchers found that when women walk  in for a pitch  they are way more likely to get  questions that are about the potential  risks of the business versus the  potential gains  even when giving identical pitches as a  male ceo  as a result even when women are given  the opportunity to pitch  they often are overlooked  this is equivalent to asking one set of  yelp reviewers in which in what way  was your food michelin star  and asking a different set of yelp  reviewers  in what way did your server screw up  your meal  the real world is obviously  a lot more subtle than that  but all of us have these unconscious  biases that impact the way that we make  decisions in this world  i wanted to take a moment to just  reflect on  why this matters so much in the world  venture capital is a tiny industry but  it is an important gatekeeper to the  types of ideas that end up existing in  this world  what kind of amazing ideas are we  missing out on because the group of  people we are backing come from such a  small homogenous and elite group  second equity returns are a huge driver  of wealth creation in this country  fifty percent of all fortune 500  companies originally vc or angel backed  so if you look at all of those  inequities all the systematic wealth  inequities in this country a lot of  those originate  at an earlier stage than you would think  at how capital is allocated  in the beginning  and to whom capital gains accrue  both on the founder and funder side  what i've said today  for women holds true for so many other  groups as well  these marginalized communities are also  in need of allies  lastly y'all are just missing out on  some crazy outsized returns  companies founded by women  are faster to reach profitability  they're more capital efficient  they literally return twice as much per  capital invested  as with most asset classes it pays to  invest in the underdog  this past year marked a record inflow of  capital into venture capital  and yet  over that same time period the  percentage of women getting allocated  that capital actually decreased  it pisses me off in so many ways to see  the numbers slide backwards  from three percent to something even  lower than three percent  yet there is also so much to be  optimistic about last year also marked  one of the biggest ipos ever by a female  founder in stitch fix  and one of the largest funding rounds  ever in women's health which has just  been an area that has been so  systematically under invested in for  generations  we still have so far to go to get to 50  but we have so much to be proud of and  to celebrate as well  i am tired and sick of waiting on the  sidelines for the numbers to change  i am in a room of movers and shakers and  i know that if we commit to this  we can change it  i'd love to re-center again  on the things that really matter and the  things that we can do  to the women in this room  who are founders  you inspire me  to be a better version of myself  thank you and i'm with you  i will be your hype woman for life  to the women who are considering  entrepreneurship but holding themselves  back for whatever reason  i believe in you  and you should own that space  to the allies in the room  we need you  to believe in us to mentor us to invest  in us  and lastly to the investors in the room  change the face of your industry  it is not enough to just hire a token  female junior team member  it is exhausting to be  the token junior  female team member  the solution is to hire at least two  but recognize that you will need to  mentor them  they will face just a immeasurable  uphill challenges as torch bearers in  the industry of venture capital  and lastly  find and fund people who don't look like  you  because it literally makes financial  sense to do so but also because it is  the right thing to do  i know that the next mark zuckerberg  will not look like him  that founder is probably in this room  go be her go work for her or go fund her  because i know she will return your  entire fund  thank you  [Music]  you

